<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <RootNamespace>Broiler.Documents.Office</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>broilerdoc-office</AssemblyName>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <RollForward>LatestMinor</RollForward>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    A console runner rather than an xunit project, for the same two reasons the
    corpus suite gives: it needs the built `broilerdoc` executable to exist
    before it runs, which is a build ordering requirement no test discovery pass
    expresses, and CI floors the `dotnet test` count to catch a build that
    silently stopped producing test assemblies. Folding several hundred
    process-level checks into that number would make the floor say less.

    It differs from the corpus suite in one way that decides everything else
    about it: it drives a second, foreign implementation. The corpus suite is
    evidence about round-trip fidelity within this component. This one is
    evidence about interchange with something that was not written here, which
    is the gap docs/corpus-suite.md openly says the corpus cannot close.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Linked rather than copied. Both files describe how a check is spawned and
    reported, and two definitions of that would drift - the corpus suite has
    already paid for one such drift, where CheckResult's own documentation
    promised an `only` option the runner never grew. They keep their original
    namespace, so the files here carry `using Broiler.Documents.Corpus;`. That
    is slightly odd to read, and it is the price of one definition.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\Broiler.Documents.Corpus\BroilerDocTool.cs" Link="BroilerDocTool.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\Broiler.Documents.Corpus\CheckResult.cs" Link="CheckResult.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Build ordering only. ReferenceOutputAssembly is false because this project
    calls the tool through its command line and never links against it: the
    contract under test is the executable's, and a runner holding a reference to
    the assembly could accidentally test the code instead of the contract.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Broiler.Documents.Cli\Broiler.Documents.Cli.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
